messages:
alex.r: Quick question — we're sketching envelope flow for multi-cloud. Do folks prefer caching the decrypted data key in app memory (short TTL) vs calling KMS on every request? main constraints: KMS quota, audit granularity, incident blast radius.

maria.s: Prefer caching with a short TTL (e.g. 30s-5m depending on traffic). Pattern: store encrypted DEK (wrapped by KEK) in DB, fetch and unwrap once per cache miss, keep plaintext DEK only in process memory with mlock/secure heap. Rewrap on KEK rotation.

dan: +1 but add hard limits. If you cache plaintext DEKs in memory you must assume in-process compromise is possible. Use process isolation (one encryption-only worker), drop privileges, and zero memory on TTL expiry. Also consider HSM-backed KMS for KEK ops.

priya: Operationally, we should add call-slimming: request coalescing + jittered backoff + circuit-breaker. If many requests for same object come in during a cold window, let the first decrypt and others wait on a promise. Also emit metrics: kms.decrypt.count, cache.hit/miss, cache.evictions.

alex.r: Good re: audit. If we cache, does that reduce helpfulness of audit logs? we want to know which service account caused decrypts for forensics.

maria.s: Auditing still works. KMS Decrypt calls are logged (who, key, timestamp). Cache hits don't hit KMS so you lose per-request records — but that tradeoff is expected. Compensating control: log local access events (service acct, object id, cache fetch vs KMS decrypt) to our centralized audit stream so you can correlate.

dan: Another trick: store an HMAC/nonce of the plaintext DEK server-side (not the plaintext). When you serve from cache, log the HMAC match event instead of a KMS decrypt. That way you can still detect unusual cache use without leaking keys.

priya: Pseudocode for get_dek (coalescing + ttl):
```
async function get_dek(obj_id) {
  if (cache.has(obj_id) && !stale(cache[obj_id])) return cache[obj_id];
  if (inflight[obj_id]) return await inflight[obj_id];
  inflight[obj_id] = (async () => {
    const wrapped = await db.fetchWrappedKey(obj_id);
    const plain = await kms.decrypt(wrapped, { keyId: KEK_ID }); // counts against KMS quota
    cache.set(obj_id, { key: plain, expires: now()+TTL });
    delete inflight[obj_id];
    return plain;
  })();
  return await inflight[obj_id];
}
```

alex.r: For multi-cloud — AWS KMS vs GCP KMS vs Azure Key Vault — do we centralize KEK management or keep per-cloud KEKs?

maria.s: For data residency and blast radius, keep KEKs per cloud/region. Use a canonical key identifier mapping in control plane that tells the runtime which KEK to use. We can offer a cross-wrap tool for migrations.

dan: Also watch out for different API semantics: GCP has CMEK nuances on rewrap and IAM bindings differ. Do not assume identical IAM primitives — design RBAC shim.

priya: RBAC notes: give service accounts minimal perms. Typical separation: (1) runtime role: kms:Decrypt / decrypt equivalent, (2) operator role: rewrap/rotate permissions, (3) admin: create/delete keys. Avoid granting rotate to runtime. Also rotate keys via a service with stricter audit & MFA.

kms-bot: FYI — current KMS quotas: AWS decrypt 1200/s per account (varies by region). If you exceed, you'll see ThrottlingException. See https://example-kms-quotas.local/docs

oncall-bot: Past incident: KEK rewrap job caused a spike of decrypts and overwhelmed the region -> degraded inference endpoints. Mitigation used: pause background rewraps, fallback to older wrapped keys, and scoped rollout (canary), add 10s jitter between rewraps.

alex.r: That incident is exactly the scenario I fear. So plan: background rewrap paced via rate limiter + random jitter + per-key lock, and have a rollback/unwrapped-key-cache to serve during rewrap. Agreed?

maria.s: Agreed. Also pre-warm caches in a rolling fashion after rewrap so you don't create a thundering herd.

dan: One more security note: keep rotation metadata immutable and auditable (who triggered, start/end times). Store that in our audit DB and include rewrap batch ids.

priya: Action items:
- Create RBAC matrix for runtime vs operator vs admin (PR by Tue)
- Implement inflight coalescing + metrics (work ticket)
- Add local audit events for cache usage (log schema)
- Design rewrap pacing algorithm + canary plan

alex.r: Thanks everyone. I'll open the PR for the RBAC matrix and a short runbook for rewrap incidents. @priya @dan can you review the runbook once I draft it? :thumbsup:

maria.s: Happy to review.

dan: I'll pair on the runbook and the secure memory checklist.
